{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PROGRESS — plan-review certified, ps2 green, ps3 in flight.\n\n## Attestation now certified\n\nrebar review-plan --status: {ok: true, verdict: certified, currency_basis: file_impact, verified_at_sha 990d102a}. It took four rounds and the failures are worth recording, because none of them found a defect in the implementation:\n\n1. STALE HEAD. The first PASS signed against origin/main while I rebased mid-review, so it was stale on arrival. Do not move the worktree HEAD while review-plan is running.\n2. PLAN LAGGED CODE (AC4). The reviewer blocked on the plan naming gerrit-review-action's 'comment-only: true' as the delivery mechanism, correctly reasoning it cannot carry text — comment-only is a LABEL switch, not a message switch, so it re-posts the same canned '<STATUS>: <url>'. The code had used a dedicated SSH 'gerrit review --message' step since the first patchset; only the description was stale. It also cited ADR-0023 (CI account is SSH-key-only, no HTTP password), which independently rules out a REST path.\n3. PLAN UNDER-SPECIFIED (mechanics). Blocked on not saying how annotations reach summarize(), and on a raw ssh defaulting to port 22 (closed on the Gerrit host) without key/known_hosts installation. Again the implementation already did all of it. Rather than assert it in prose I made each detail verifiable — six new workflow-parsing tests now pin port 29418, the chmod 600 key + known_hosts install, the ^[1-9][0-9]*$ change/patchset guard, the if: always() key removal, and both API fetches — and added AC9/AC10 to match.\n4. DESCRIPTION TOO LONG. 8001 chars against an 8000 admission cap, self-inflicted by fixing (2) and (3) with more prose. Rewrote rather than shaved one character: 8001 -> 6247, keeping every load-bearing specific and cutting redundancy. Detail belongs in code and tests; the plan carries only what a reviewer needs to judge feasibility.\n\nLesson for the next agent: the gate reviews the PLAN, never your diff. Update the description the moment the design changes — recording a deviation in a comment (as I did) does not reach the reviewer.\n\n## Gerrit\n\n- ps2: LLM-Review +1 AND Verified +1 (CI run 31575196657 SUCCESS). The earlier Verified -1 was a real failure in my own change, not a known-flake: the comment-hygiene guard rejected a raw GitHub run id in a test docstring, which failed the lint job AND all four pytest cells from one root cause. Fixed by replacing the id with durable prose. No recheck would have helped.\n- The earlier LLM-Review -1 was bugfix-size-attestation: 268 non-test lines over the 150-line bugfix floor with no attestation on this ticket. Cleared by the signed PASS above.\n- ps3 pushed (rebased onto 990d102a) and awaiting votes. I did NOT submit the green ps2: it predates the six tests that enforce AC9/AC10, so landing it would have closed this ticket against acceptance criteria nothing verified.\n\nLocal gates at the ps3 tree: 17/17 workflow tests, full targeted suite green, check_verify_gate_parity.py OK, check_comment_hygiene.py clean, make lint (ruff + zizmor + actionlint) and make typecheck clean."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786522685723363001,"uuid":"33ab7327-5ccd-495d-accb-247bc55ca4b5"}